        "id": "def-affine-space-and-displacement",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Affine spaces over a real vector space and displacement vectors",
        "statement": "Let V be a real vector space. An affine space over V is a nonempty set A together with a map +: A x V -> A such that (a) (p+v)+w = p+(v+w) and p+0 = p for all p in A and v,w in V, and (b) for all p,q in A there is exactly one v in V with p+v = q. That unique v is written q-p and called the displacement from p to q. Then p+(q-p) = q, p-p = 0, p+0 = p, (q-p)+(r-q) = r-p, and for each p in A the map v |-> p+v is a bijection A -> A. If V is 4-dimensional and E: R^4 -> V is a linear isomorphism, then x(p) := E^{-1}(p-o) is a bijection A -> R^4 for each o in A; such maps are the affine coordinate systems on A.",
        "strategy": "The uniqueness clause (b) is what makes q-p well defined. The identities follow from the action axioms and uniqueness: p+(q-p) = q is the defining property of q-p; p-p = 0 and p+0 = p come from p+0 = p; and p+[(q-p)+(r-q)] = [p+(q-p)]+(r-q) = q+(r-q) = r = p+(r-p) gives (q-p)+(r-q) = r-p by uniqueness. For each p, v |-> p+v is surjective by (b) and injective because an equality p+v = p+w and the action axioms give p+(v-w) = p = p+0, so v-w = 0 by (b) applied at (p,p).",
        "deps": [
