      {
        "id": "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Lorentz forms of inertia (3,1,0) on a 4-dimensional real vector space",
        "statement": "Let V be a 4-dimensional real vector space. A Lorentz form on V is a symmetric bilinear form eta: V x V -> R that is nondegenerate (both radicals are {0}) and has inertia (3,1,0): in some (equivalently, by Sylvester's law of inertia, in every) basis diagonalizing eta there are exactly three positive diagonal entries, exactly one negative diagonal entry, and no zero diagonal entry. The associated quadratic form is q(v) := eta(v,v). The pair (V,eta) is called a Lorentz vector space of signature (-,+,+,+).",
        "strategy": "Nondegeneracy is the statement that the radicals vanish; for finite-dimensional V it is equivalent to invertibility of the matrix of eta in any basis and hence to the absence of zero diagonal entries in every diagonalizing basis (the radical is the kernel of the associated map V -> V*, so its dimension is dim V minus the rank). The inertia (3,1,0) is the Sylvester normal form diag(I_3,-I_1,0_0) of the definition of definiteness/inertia; Sylvester's law makes it basis-independent. No positive-definite structure on V is assumed.",
        "deps": [
