        "id": "lem-lorentz-form-has-orthonormal-basis",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Every Lorentz form admits an ordered basis with matrix diag(-1,1,1,1)",
        "statement": "Let eta be a Lorentz form on a 4-dimensional real vector space V. There is an ordered basis (e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3) of V with eta(e_a,e_b) = diag(-1,1,1,1)_{ab} for all a,b. Such a basis is called eta-orthonormal; in every eta-orthonormal ordered basis exactly one basis vector is timelike and the other three are spacelike, and the count is the inertia (3,1,0) of eta.",
        "strategy": "Sylvester's law of inertia supplies a basis in which the matrix of eta is the normal form diag(I_p,-I_q,0_r) with (p,q,r) the inertia of eta. The definition of a Lorentz form gives (p,q,r) = (3,1,0), so the matrix is diag(1,1,1,-1); reindexing the basis puts the single negative entry in the first slot and yields diag(-1,1,1,1). Since inertia is independent of the diagonalizing basis, every eta-orthonormal basis exhibits the same count.",
