        "id": "def-lorentz-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "eta-orthogonality and orthogonal complements in a Lorentz vector space",
        "statement": "Let (V,eta) be a Lorentz vector space. Vectors u,v are eta-orthogonal, written u orthogonal v, if eta(u,v) = 0. For a subset S of V the orthogonal complement is S^perp := {v in V : eta(v,s) = 0 for all s in S}, and for a vector u one writes u^perp := {u}^perp. For all subsets S the set S^perp is a linear subspace of V, S is contained in (S^perp)^perp, and S^perp = (span S)^perp; in particular u belongs to u^perp exactly when eta(u,u) = 0. Unlike a positive-definite inner product, a nonzero vector can be orthogonal to itself.",
        "strategy": "Bilinearity gives that S^perp is closed under addition and scalar multiplication (each condition eta(.,s) = 0 is linear), and the containments and the equality S^perp = (span S)^perp follow by linearity in the second argument. The final sentence is witnessed by the classification: any nonzero null vector v satisfies eta(v,v) = 0, so v lies in v^perp.",
