      {
        "id": "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Time orientation, future and past timelike/null/causal vectors, and the closed cones",
        "statement": "Let (V,eta) be a Lorentz vector space and let T := {v in V : eta(v,v) < 0} be its timelike set. By the components lemma T has exactly two connected components; a time orientation of (V,eta) is a choice of one of them. Fixing such a choice and writing C^+ for the chosen component and C^- := T \\ C^+ for the other, call v future timelike if v is in C^+, past timelike if v is in C^-, future null if v is nonzero, eta(v,v) = 0 and v lies in the closure cl(C^+), past null if v is nonzero, eta(v,v) = 0 and v lies in cl(C^-), future causal if it is future timelike or future null, and past causal if it is past timelike or past null. The sets cl(C^+) and cl(C^-) are the future and past closed cones; they are convex cones meeting only at 0, and a causal vector is future or past but not both.",
        "strategy": "Well-definedness rests on the components lemma: T has exactly two components, so 'a choice of one of them' names a definite pair {C^+,C^-}. The claimed properties of the closures (convexity, the intersection {0}, and exclusivity of the future and past causal sets) are proved in the components lemma via the orthonormal coordinate description {v^0 >= |v_vec|} and {v^0 <= -|v_vec|} of the two closed cones.",
