      {
        "id": "lem-timelike-cone-has-two-convex-components",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "The timelike set has exactly two convex components, described in orthonormal coordinates",
        "statement": "Let (V,eta) be a Lorentz vector space and let (e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3) be an eta-orthonormal ordered basis; write v = v^0 e_0 + v^1 e_1 + v^2 e_2 + v^3 e_3 and |v_vec| := sqrt((v^1)^2+(v^2)^2+(v^3)^2). Then eta(v,v) = -(v^0)^2 + |v_vec|^2, and the timelike set T = {v : eta(v,v) < 0} satisfies T = T_+ union T_- with T_+ := {v : v^0 > |v_vec|} and T_- := {v : v^0 < -|v_vec|}. The sets T_+ and T_- are nonempty, disjoint, open in V, convex and path-connected, and they are exactly the two connected components of T; in every eta-orthonormal basis whose first vector is timelike the same description holds, with the two displayed sets possibly interchanged. Moreover cl(T_+) = {v : v^0 >= |v_vec|}, cl(T_-) = {v : v^0 <= -|v_vec|}, and cl(T_+) intersection cl(T_-) = {0}.",
        "strategy": "In the orthonormal basis, eta(v,v) = -(v^0)^2 + sum_i (v^i)^2, so eta(v,v) < 0 iff |v^0| > |v_vec|, which is the displayed union; the two sets are disjoint and nonempty (e_0 lies in the first, -e_0 in the second). Convexity of T_+: for u,w in T_+ and t in [0,1], (tu+(1-t)w)^0 = t u^0 + (1-t) w^0 > t|u_vec| + (1-t)|w_vec| >= |t u_vec + (1-t) w_vec| by the triangle inequality; T_- is handled by applying this to -v. Convex sets are path-connected, and T_+ and T_- are open (they are the preimages of (0,inf) under the continuous maps v |-> v^0 - |v_vec| and v |-> -v^0 - |v_vec|). Since T is the disjoint union of two nonempty relatively open connected sets, it is disconnected and these are its two components: any connected subset of T lies in one of them, because a connected subset meeting both would give a separation, while a path in T cannot change the sign of the continuous function v^0 (which is nowhere zero on T) by the intermediate value theorem. The closures are computed by continuity of v^0 -/+ |v_vec|; their intersection consists of vectors with v^0 >= |v_vec| and v^0 <= -|v_vec|, hence of 0 alone. Finally, any two orthonormal bases are related by a linear isomorphism, and the components of T are intrinsic to eta, so the coordinate description agrees with the components up to the naming of T_+ and T_-.",
