      {
        "id": "def-sr-affine-minkowski-model",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space (the spacetime primitives)",
        "statement": "An oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space is a quadruple (A,V,eta,C^+) consisting of: a nonempty affine space A over a 4-dimensional real vector space V (def-affine-space-and-displacement); a Lorentz form eta on V (def-lorentz-form-and-inertia); an orientation of V, i.e. a positive ray of the determinant line Lambda^4 V, fixed once and for all (def-determinant-line-orientation-of-a-finite-dimensional-real-vector-space); and a time orientation C^+ of (V,eta) (def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets). The points of A are called events; q-p is the displacement from p to q; the interval of p,q in A is I(p,q) := eta(q-p,q-p); and p,q are timelike, null, spacelike or causally related when q-p is timelike, null, spacelike or causal, future-related when q-p is future causal, and past-related when q-p is past causal (def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors, def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets). No point of A is distinguished: for every v in V the translation p |-> p+v is a bijection of A preserving all the structure except the labelling of points. All later structure of this framework (frames, charts, tensor fields, worldlines) is built on this model, and no derived physical quantity is part of it.",
        "strategy": "The definition is a structure specification: the affine action, the form, the orientation and the time orientation are separately defined objects, and the definition only bundles them and fixes the terminology (events, displacement, interval, causal relations). Well-definedness of the bundled data requires the existence and uniqueness statements supplied by the cited items: affine coordinates are available by the affine-space definition, the orthonormal basis needed later exists by the orthonormal-basis lemma, and the two components of the timelike set needed to choose C^+ exist by the components lemma. The translation invariance claim follows from the affine axioms: displacements are unchanged by translations, and translations have identity linear part, so they preserve eta, the orientation and C^+.",
        "deps": [
          "def-affine-space-and-displacement",
          "def-lorentz-form-and-inertia",
          "def-causal-classification-of-lorentz-vectors",
          "def-time-orientation-and-future-causal-sets",
