        "title": "The tangent space of the affine Minkowski model at any event is canonically V",
        "statement": "Let (A,V,eta,C^+) be an oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space with its smooth structure and let p be an event. For a smooth germ [f] at p define Phi_p(v)([f]) := (d/dt)|_{t=0} f(p+tv). Then Phi_p: V -> T_pA is a linear isomorphism of V onto the tangent space T_pA; in particular T_pA is canonically identified with V, independently of any chart. If x is an inertial chart and v = v^0 e_0 + v^1 e_1 + v^2 e_2 + v^3 e_3, then the coordinate derivations at p correspond under this identification to partial_a = e_a, i.e. Phi_p(v) = sum_a v^a partial_a|_p.",
        "strategy": "Well-definedness: for a smooth germ [f] the curve c(t) := p + t v has, in the inertial chart x, the coordinate representation x(p) + t u with u = E^{-1} v, hence is smooth; therefore f o c is smooth near 0 and the derivative at 0 exists. Differentiation of products of smooth functions gives Phi_p(v)([fg]) = f(p) Phi_p(v)([g]) + g(p) Phi_p(v)([f]) by the product rule, so each Phi_p(v) is a derivation, and Phi_p is linear in v by the chain rule applied to t |-> x(p) + t u. Injectivity: if Phi_p(v) = 0 and x is an inertial chart at p with coordinates x^a, then applying Phi_p(v) to the germs x^a - x^a(p) gives v^a for each a, so v = 0. Bijectivity: the coordinate derivations partial_0,...,partial_3 form a basis of T_pA, so dim T_pA = 4 = dim V, and an injective linear map between these spaces is an isomorphism. Canonicity: the curve t |-> p + tv is defined by the affine structure alone, so Phi_p does not depend on the chart; the final display follows from the coordinate computation in the chart.",
