        "title": "The Euclidean spatial structure and cross product of an inertial frame",
        "statement": "Let (A,V,eta,C^+) be an oriented time-oriented affine Minkowski space and let (o;e_0,e_1,e_2,e_3) be an inertial frame. Put S := span(e_1,e_2,e_3) = {w in V : eta(e_0,w) = 0} and let delta be the restriction of eta to S. Then delta is a positive-definite symmetric bilinear form on S (the Euclidean spatial form of the frame), |w| := sqrt(delta(w,w)) is a norm on S, and (e_1,e_2,e_3) is a delta-orthonormal positively oriented ordered basis of S. Writing w = w^1 e_1 + w^2 e_2 + w^3 e_3, define the vector product w x z as the unique element of S with components ((w x z)^1,(w x z)^2,(w x z)^3) = (w^2 z^3 - w^3 z^2, w^3 z^1 - w^1 z^3, w^1 z^2 - w^2 z^1). Then delta(w x z, y) equals the determinant of the coordinate matrix with columns w,z,y for every y in S, and |w x z|^2 = |w|^2 |z|^2 - delta(w,z)^2. All these objects belong to the positive spatial three-space of the named inertial frame: they are equivariant under orientation-preserving spatial rotations of the frame and are not invariant under a general Lorentz map. The invariant structure of the model remains eta alone.",
